  "id": "Compound::DB01394 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Colchicine may interact with Cyp2c9 through multiple indirect pathways, primarily involving the binding of certain compounds to the enzyme. Colchicine is linked to Prostration and Steatorrhoea, both of which are caused by other substances, and these substances in turn bind to Cyp2c9. Additionally, Colchicine is classified as a Cytochrome P450 3a4 inhibitor, which includes Efavirenz, a compound that also binds to Cyp2c9. This indicates that Colchicine may influence Cyp2c9 through different mechanisms, such as direct binding or through the indirect effects of other compounds that share a common ancestor. The cumulative evidence suggests a potential role for Colchicine in modulating Cyp2c9 activity, though the exact nature of the interaction remains unclear due to the indirect pathways involved.",
